.TH INSTALL-SSH-CONFIG.SH 1 "2026-10-28" "mu2edaq-shifter-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
install-ssh-config.sh \- install the Mu2e DAQ ssh client configuration on your own computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B install-ssh-config.sh
[\fB\-U\fR \fIfnal_user\fR]
[\fB\-D\fR \fIdaq_user\fR]
[\fB\-S\fR \fIshift_user\fR]
[\fB\-C\fR \fIdcs_user\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR \fIssh_config\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIfragment\fR]
[\fB\-e\fR \fIenv_file\fR]
[\fB\-I\fR]
[\fB\-y\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBClient side.\fR Run this on your own laptop or workstation (macOS,
Linux, or Windows under Git Bash or WSL), never on a DAQ host. The
DAQ-host counterpart is
.BR install-login.sh (1).
.PP
.B install-ssh-config.sh
sets up the ssh paths to the Mu2e DAQ: the MC-2 gateway
\fImu2egateway01\fR and the hosts behind it (\fImu2e\-mgr\-*\fR,
\fImu2e\-trk*\fR, \fImu2e\-calo*\fR, \fImu2e\-dl\-*\fR,
\fImu2e\-cfo*\fR, \fImu2e\-dcs\-*\fR), the MC-1 control room and
cluster workers behind \fIg2gateway01\fR, and the HEERC/IERC teststands
behind \fImu2edaq\-gateway\fR. It does three things:
.IP 1. 4
Renders \fBconfig/ssh\-config\-mu2e.in\fR with your account names and
installs it, mode 600, as \fI~/.ssh/config_mu2e\fR.
.IP 2. 4
Adds \fBInclude ~/.ssh/config_mu2e\fR at the top of
\fI~/.ssh/config\fR, creating that file if needed. The Include must come
before every \fBHost\fR or \fBMatch\fR line, because inside a block it
only applies to hosts that block matches. If an existing Include is
inside a block, the script says which line to move it above and exits
with status 1; it does not rearrange your file.
.IP 3. 4
Records \fBNOVNC_USER\fR and \fBMU2EDAQ_KRB5_PRINCIPAL\fR in
\fI~/.mu2edaq/env\fR, where
.BR start\-novnc\-connection.sh (1)
and
.BR daq\-ssh\-check.sh (1)
read them. Other keys in that file are kept.
.PP
The fragment applies only to Mu2e host patterns. It sets \fBUser\fR,
\fBProxyJump\fR, \fBHostName\fR (short name to \fI.fnal.gov\fR) and
GSSAPI authentication and delegation, and nothing for \fBHost *\fR.
Host-key checking, agent forwarding and X11 forwarding stay whatever
your own configuration says.
.PP
Every file that would change is first copied to
\fI<file>.bak.<YYYYmmddHHMMSS>\fR. Running the script again with the
same answers changes nothing and makes no backups.
.SS Prompts
When standard input is a terminal and \fB\-y\fR is not given, the
script asks for each value that was not supplied on the command line or
in the environment, offering a default in brackets:
.TP
.B Fermilab username
Your Kerberos principal is \fI<name>@FNAL.GOV\fR. It is used as the ssh
login for the noVNC portal on \fImu2e\-mgr\-01\fR and to check which
ticket is active. Default: \fBNOVNC_USER\fR from \fI~/.mu2edaq/env\fR,
else the principal shown by \fBklist\fR, else your local login name.
.TP
.B Shared DAQ account
Login on \fImu2egateway01\fR, the MC-2 hosts and the teststands.
Default \fImu2edaq\fR.
.TP
.B Shared shift account
Login on the MC-1 control room, \fIg2gateway01\fR and the cluster
workers. Default \fImu2eshift\fR.
.TP
.B DCS account
Login on \fImu2e\-dcs\-*\fR. Default \fImu2edcs\fR.
.PP
Your principal must be listed in the \fI.k5login\fR of each shared
account you use; the installer cannot check that, but
.BR daq\-ssh\-check.sh (1)
can. Account names must be lower-case letters, digits, \fB.\fR,
\fB_\fR or \fB\-\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
Configuration precedence is command line, then environment variable,
then .env file, then the prompt (or its default when not prompting).
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-fnal\-user " " \fINAME\fR
Fermilab username. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_FNAL_USER\fR.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-daq\-user " " \fINAME\fR
Shared DAQ account. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_SSH_DAQ_USER\fR.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-shift\-user " " \fINAME\fR
Shared shift account. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_SSH_SHIFT_USER\fR.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-dcs\-user " " \fINAME\fR
DCS account. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_SSH_DCS_USER\fR.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-ssh\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
ssh configuration to add the Include to. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_SSH_CONFIG\fR. Default: \fI~/.ssh/config\fR.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Where to install the rendered fragment. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_SSH_FRAGMENT\fR. Default: \fI~/.ssh/config_mu2e\fR.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-env\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Personal settings file. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_CLIENT_ENV\fR. Default:
\fI~/.mu2edaq/env\fR.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-no\-include
Install the fragment and settings but leave the ssh configuration alone.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Never prompt; use the defaults for anything not supplied.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be done and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/ssh\-config\-mu2e.in
The template, looked for beside the script, under
\fB$MU2EDAQ_ROOT\fR\fI/config\fR, then under
\fI<prefix>/share/mu2edaq/config\fR for a CMake install.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config_mu2e
The rendered fragment.
.TP
.I ~/.mu2edaq/env
Personal settings for the client-side commands.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error or a misplaced Include, 2 on a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive install, then check the result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git clone https://github.com/Mu2e/mu2edaq-shifter-tools.git
cd mu2edaq-shifter-tools
\&./install-ssh-config.sh
kinit jdoe@FNAL.GOV
scripts/daq-ssh-check.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Unattended, with a non-default DAQ account:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./install-ssh-config.sh \-y \-U jdoe \-D mu2etrk
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR daq\-ssh\-check.sh (1),
.BR start\-novnc\-connection.sh (1),
.BR install\-login.sh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5)
